Option 1: Argo CD UI
1. Open the Application in the UI
2. Click History and Rollback (clock icon)
3. You'll see a list of previous revisions with their commit SHAs and timestamps
4. Select the revision you want to rollback to
5. Click Rollback

Note: this only works if Argo CD has app history recorded — it doesn't work well if you've
disabled revision history or if the app uses Automated sync with self-heal aggressively re-
syncing to HEAD.

Option 2: CLI (argocd CLI)

View history:

argocd app history <app-name>

This lists revisions like:

ID  DATE                       REVISION
0   2024-05-01 10:00:00 +0000 UTC  a1b2c3d
1   2024-05-02 14:30:00 +0000 UTC  d4e5f6g
2   2024-05-03 09:15:00 +0000 UTC  h7i8j9k

Rollback to a specific revision ID:

argocd app rollback <app-name> <ID>

Example:

argocd app rollback my-app 1

Option 3: GitOps way (recommended, more "correct")

Since Argo CD is declarative and syncs from Git, the proper GitOps rollback is to revert the
commit in your Git repo (the source of truth), not fight Argo CD directly:

git revert <bad-commit-sha>
git push

Argo CD will then auto-sync (or you manually sync) to the reverted state. This is preferred
because:

• The argocd app rollback CLI/UI method causes drift — Argo CD's desired state (Git)
  still points to the bad version, so if auto-sync + self-heal is on, it'll snap back to the bad
  state again
• Git-based rollback keeps your Git history as the single source of truth, consistent with
  GitOps principles

Important caveat

If your app has automated sync with self-heal enabled:

syncPolicy:
  automated:
    selfHeal: true

Then argocd app rollback will get immediately overridden/re-synced back to whatever's in
Git — because Argo CD detects "drift" from Git and corrects it. In that case, you must fix Git
first (revert the commit), not just rollback via CLI/UI.

Quick decision guide:

• Need immediate rollback + auto-sync is off or manual → argocd app rollback is fine
• Auto-sync + self-heal is on → revert in Git, let Argo CD resync
• Want a clean audit trail → always prefer Git revert
